xnu 0375: name the function that creates and fills a picture surface

Measured 2026-11-19: the window server creates the 640 x 480 surface the Library
tile is drawn from, takes one write lock and leaves it all zero, while the layer
in the app holds the picture's four coloured quadrants. The fill that loses the
pixels is therefore inside backboardd's CoreAnimation, and `0374` only names the
process.

This prints six frames of the frame-pointer chain, in the user space of the
caller, at the first create, lock and unlock of a picture surface, with the
shared cache slide on the same line so `tools/dsc-va.py CACHE --slide 0x...`
names each frame in QuartzCore. The lock and unlock are the fill's own
brackets; the create says which function allocates the surface the fill leaves
empty.

Follow-up 2026-11-19: the decompressed symbols cache names the function:
`CA::Render::Image::retain_iosurface() const` only creates the surface and its
lock/unlock move the image's seed; the upload (`CA::OGL::GLESContext::retain_image`)
reads the image's data pointer through `CA::Render::Image::copy_cgimage()`, and
that pointer is zero. The loss is the app-to-server image data, not the surface.


